The application should be developed **module by module**.

The initial order is:

```text
1. Project Foundation
        ↓
2. Authentication
        ↓
3. User
        ↓
4. Landing / Explore
        ↓
5. Cities
        ↓
6. Tours
        ↓
7. News
        ↓
8. Publisher
        ↓
9. Admin
        ↓
10. Media / Image Processing
        ↓
11. Integration / Testing / Polish
```

Each module will later receive its own detailed requirements.

For each module we will define:

```text
Module
  ↓
Requirements
  ↓
Domain model
  ↓
Database changes
  ↓
Application logic
  ↓
API
  ↓
Frontend
  ↓
Authorization
  ↓
Testing
```

---

# 19. MVP Definition

The first meaningful version of Country is complete when a visitor can:

1. Open Country.
2. Explore available travel content.
3. Browse cities.
4. Browse tours.
5. Read news.
6. Open content details.
7. Register.
8. Log in.
9. Access their user area.
10. A Publisher can log in and manage publishing content.
11. An Admin can access the administration area.
12. The system persists its core data in PostgreSQL.
13. Authentication and authorization work across frontend and backend.

The exact behavior of each item will be defined later in the module-specific requirements.

---

# 20. Current Project Boundary

At this stage, Country should be understood as:

> **A structured travel-content platform where people discover destinations and travel information, while authenticated publishers provide the content and administrators manage the platform.**

This document is a **project map**, not the final specification.

The next step is to take one module at a time and turn it into an actual requirement set.
